# These fixtures are asserted byte-for-byte against real runtime output
# (FullPipelineGoldenTest, FixtureDriftTest) or parsed by a fixed-width parser
# that depends on exact line lengths (the ingestion-service tests). Their line
# endings are part of the contract and intentionally differ from each other, so
# git must never normalize them: a clone with core.autocrlf=true would otherwise
# rewrite them, silently breaking the CSV byte-equality assertions and the
# fixed-width parser alike.
#
# Input.txt is marked -text, not "text eol=crlf". Both check out CRLF, but
# eol=crlf also means "store LF in the repo" -- and these blobs were committed
# CRLF, so git saw every clone as having four modified files on arrival and
# wanted to renormalize them. Input.txt is supplied input that must not be
# rewritten, so -text is the honest declaration: store the bytes verbatim and
# convert nothing in either direction.
#
# Output.csv is generated by this project rather than supplied to it, and its
# blobs are already LF, so eol=lf is consistent there and stays.
sample-output/Output.csv text eol=lf
sample-data/Input.txt -text
processing-service/src/test/resources/Output.csv text eol=lf
processing-service/src/test/resources/Input.txt -text
ingestion-service/src/test/resources/Input.txt -text
common/src/test/resources/Input.txt -text
